= Taxonomy =

== Overview ==
A '''taxonomy''' is an organized system of categories or concepts, commonly arranged through broader–narrower or parent–child relationships.

Taxonomies support:
* classification;
* navigation;
* indexing;
* search;
* communication;
* information architecture.

The term comes historically from biological classification but is now used broadly in information systems and knowledge organization.

== Hierarchical organization ==
A simple taxonomy can be a tree:

<pre>
Vehicle
├─ Land vehicle
│  ├─ Car
│  └─ Bicycle
└─ Watercraft
   └─ Boat
</pre>

This is easy to browse.

But many real taxonomies are polyhierarchical: one concept can have several broader parents.

Then the structure is a directed graph rather than a strict tree.

== Broader and narrower concepts ==
Knowledge-organization systems often use broader/narrower relations.

"Dog" can be narrower than "Mammal."

But broader/narrower is not always formal logical subclasshood.

In subject taxonomies, a broader concept may reflect:
* generic kind;
* part–whole relation;
* topic inclusion;
* administrative grouping.

Automated reasoning should therefore know which relation is intended.

== Taxonomy and classification ==
[[Classification]] assigns items to categories.

A taxonomy organizes the categories/concepts available for that assignment.

A library taxonomy can exist even when no new document is currently being classified.

== Taxonomy and ontology ==
An [[Ontology]] generally supports richer semantics than a taxonomy.

An ontology may include:
* many relation types;
* formal axioms;
* property constraints;
* identity;
* disjointness;
* inference rules or logical consequences.

A taxonomy is often lighter weight and optimized for organization rather than logical reasoning.

== Taxonomy and thesaurus ==
A thesaurus normally includes controlled terms plus relationships such as:
* broader term;
* narrower term;
* related term;
* preferred/non-preferred labels.

A taxonomy is often more hierarchy-centered.

In practice the boundary is not sharp.

ISO 25964 and SKOS treat these as related families of knowledge organization systems.

== Biological taxonomy ==
Biological taxonomy classifies organisms into named groups such as species, genera and families.

This scientific use has its own rules, nomenclature and evolutionary debates.

It should not be treated as the model for every information-system taxonomy.

== Faceted taxonomy ==
A faceted system uses independent dimensions.

For example, a product can be organized by:
* material;
* purpose;
* manufacturer;
* price range.

Facets avoid forcing every distinction into one giant hierarchy.

They are useful when items naturally belong to several independent classification dimensions.

== Polyhierarchy ==
Polyhierarchy allows one concept to have multiple broader concepts.

Example:

<pre>
Bioinformatics
→ Computer science
→ Biology
</pre>

This can better reflect interdisciplinary structure.

It also complicates inheritance and navigation.

== Taxonomy maintenance ==
Useful taxonomy maintenance includes:

* stable concept identifiers;
* preferred labels;
* synonyms;
* scope notes;
* change history;
* redirects/deprecations;
* merge/split tracking;
* mapping between schemes.

Changing a label should not necessarily change concept identity.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki uses categories and portals for public navigation while avoiding one giant taxonomy as the ontology.

This keeps domain browsing separate from entity type and semantic relations.

== Epistemic notes ==
Taxonomies are models of organization. Their structure can reflect scientific theory, practical retrieval needs or institutional convention. Hierarchy should not be mistaken automatically for metaphysical truth.

